{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2368 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d4e1af93e0c16dd402b42a2b6dbe3f3b8eb57435416891b182abe10dfdd256e5" :edb/db-before-t 2367 :edb/db-after-t 2368 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535680 50 "2026-10-14T21:01:13.954Z" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059220 1078 17592186059221 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059518 1071 "If $\\log_7 (2x + 1) = 1$, then what is $x$?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059518 1071 "If $\\log_7 (2x + 1) = 1,$ then what is $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059518 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7$, so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{7} (2x + 1) &= 1 \\\\\n7^{\\log_{7} (2x + 1)} &= 7^{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 1 &= 7 \\\\\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059518 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(2x+1) & =1 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(2x+1)} & ={7}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059592 1071 "Given that $2\\log_5 (x-3)+1 = 5$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059592 1071 "Given that $2\\log_5 (x-3)+1 = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059592 1074 "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\log_{5} (x - 3) + 1 &= 5 \\\\\n2\\log_{5} (x - 3) &= 4 \\\\\n\\log_{5} (x - 3) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5$, so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{5} (x - 3) &= 2 \\\\\n5^{\\log_{5} (x - 3)} &= 5^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 3 &= 5^{2} \\\\\nx - 3 &= 25 \\\\\nx &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059592 1074 "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{5}⁡(x-3)+1 & =5 \\\\ 2{log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =4 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x-3)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x-3 & =25 \\\\ x & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059593 1071 "If $\\log_2 x = 5$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059593 1071 "If $\\log_2 x = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059593 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2$, so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{2} x &= 5 \\\\\n2^{\\log_{2} x} &= 2^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n2^{\\log_2 x} = x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5} \\\\\nx &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059593 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡x & =5 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡x} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $2^{\\log_2 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5} \\\\ x & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059594 1071 "If $7\\log_6{x} - 3\\log_6{x} = 2$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059594 1071 "If $7\\log_6{x} - 3\\log_6{x} = 2,$ then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059594 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7\\log_{6} x - 3\\log_{6} x &= 2 \\\\\n(7 - 3)\\log_{6} x &= 2 \\\\\n4\\log_{6} x &= 2 \\\\\n\\log_{6} x &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6$, so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{6} x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n6^{\\log_{6} x} &= 6^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n6^{\\log_6 x} = x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 6^{1/2} \\\\\nx &= \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186059594 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{6}⁡x-3{log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (7-3){log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 4{log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6,$ so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {6}^{{log}_{6}⁡x} & ={6}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $6^{\\log_6 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={6}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059695 1038 17592186526054 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059695 1038 17592186526104 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059793 1038 17592186526032 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059793 1038 17592186526082 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059794 1038 17592186526024 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059795 1038 17592186526019 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186059909 1050 480.0 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186060433 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186060433 1071 "If $\\log_{4} x = 3$, then $x =$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186060433 1071 "If ${log}_{4}⁡x=3$, then $x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186060433 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use a base of $4$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡x & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{3} \\\\ x & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186060906 1064 "Suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\n\\log_3 x = 4\n$$\n\nCan we determine $x$?\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x$, we must exponentiate both sides of this equation.\n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3$, so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} x &= 4 \\\\\n3^{(\\log_{3} x)} &= 3^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\n3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 3^{4} \\\\\nx &= 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186060906 1064 "Suppose we are given the equation $\\log_3 x = 4.$ Can we determine $x?$\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x,$ we must exponentiate both sides of this equation. \n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {3}^{({log}_{3}⁡x)} & ={3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x.$ Therefore, we get   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186064307 1038 17592186525994 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186064307 1038 17592186526080 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186064308 1038 17592186526033 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186064308 1038 17592186526084 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078360 1071 "The graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}2)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}3)$ - Translate right by $6$ units\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 12$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhat is a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078360 1071 "The graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\textrm{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n- $(\\textrm{T}2)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n- $(\\textrm{T}3)$ - Translate right by $6$ units\n- $(\\textrm{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 12$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhat is a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186078360 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5 = f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nTo get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nwe could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)$: Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)$: Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right)$ right by $6$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)$: Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)+5$.\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$$\n(\\textrm{T}1) \\to (\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2)\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186078360 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac x2-3\\right)+5 = f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nTo get $y=f\\left(\\dfrac 12(x-6)\\right)+5,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)\\mathbin{:}$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac x2 \\right)$ right by $6$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f\\left(\\dfrac12{(x-6)}\\right)+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$$\n(\\textrm{T}1) \\to (\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2).\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078361 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)-1$.\n\n![](images/69/69e2a7112819db4536ea902c236c7d272fb8fb5ada1d114a59dcea5de212d4d5.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186078361 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)-1.$\n\n![](images/69/69e2a7112819db4536ea902c236c7d272fb8fb5ada1d114a59dcea5de212d4d5.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078361 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x+2\\right) -1 = f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x)$.\n\n![](images/b9/b9f7d5c0b8014bc3e37171efb5bcc6422573cb4dc3a3a3ed783ca10385fd9a86.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e0469b84d60010a5cd78ee9b575aec441180b615a2db5587099d65e4bf3496.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)-1$.\n\n![](images/c6/c6ebcaa8fd8d421de53231e381130967ec35a270b35786d17a0e4c9e98420a9e.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186078361 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x+2\\right) -1 = f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right) - 1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](images/b9/b9f7d5c0b8014bc3e37171efb5bcc6422573cb4dc3a3a3ed783ca10385fd9a86.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right).$\n\n![](images/d4/d4e0469b84d60010a5cd78ee9b575aec441180b615a2db5587099d65e4bf3496.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x+1)\\right)-1.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6ebcaa8fd8d421de53231e381130967ec35a270b35786d17a0e4c9e98420a9e.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078362 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5$.\n\n![](images/b3/b314de8320a82ad5a7bd395da63c6f9bd4348ee2f4fd12c13a090c20fbcdd322.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186078362 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5.$\n\n![](images/b3/b314de8320a82ad5a7bd395da63c6f9bd4348ee2f4fd12c13a090c20fbcdd322.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078362 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=2f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right)+5\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/92/92ffae3729220d6eae6cfb96fde2efc93f19b58ad38a5ba36760fb8b38ee8ced.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fc767f318d276a0178f4ceb2d2550a5141744ca2c3ed8da1f240309d3a1ca0.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/14/143a49c724c30a9e9cf432960fcf7046784a9a881182959875b9805adb0a8c5e.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7295138c2821d0d3c479ff9d98e2018bc26493ff09f41fe463cc31cee0907e.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186078362 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}x\\right).$\n\n![](images/92/92ffae3729220d6eae6cfb96fde2efc93f19b58ad38a5ba36760fb8b38ee8ced.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left({3}(x+3)\\right).$\n\n![](images/c4/c4fc767f318d276a0178f4ceb2d2550a5141744ca2c3ed8da1f240309d3a1ca0.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right).$\n\n![](images/14/143a49c724c30a9e9cf432960fcf7046784a9a881182959875b9805adb0a8c5e.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=2f\\left(3(x+3)\\right)+5.$\n\n![](images/6d/6d7295138c2821d0d3c479ff9d98e2018bc26493ff09f41fe463cc31cee0907e.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078510 1038 17592186526016 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078510 1038 17592186526069 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078511 1038 17592186526018 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186078511 1038 17592186526039 13194139535680 175921860