[](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to get the graph of ![](images/d0/d027e035caec097035ee8f20e49d28dadb156445265efe674460854cce152406.svg)\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n\n- Finally, translate it up by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit to get the graph of ![](images/f6/f6d6c8ff90e58f882bbd48d3b52f37f8c1657a5a45218d31c481c2a400e25ac3.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186079675 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$, how can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2x-4\\right)+1 \\,\n$$\n\nIn this particular situation, we have to combine several operations.\n\nTo perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must apply the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nIn this case, we begin by factoring out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny = f\\left(2x-4\\right) +1 = f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNow, to plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right) +1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x)$.\n\n![](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n- Finally, translate it up by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2(x-2)\\right)+1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186085124 1075 17592186525416 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186085124 1078 17592186525416 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186085125 1075 17592186525515 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186085125 1078 17592186525515 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186086454 1075 17592186086455 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186086454 1075 17592186086456 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186086454 1075 17592186086457 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186086454 1075 17592186086458 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186086454 1075 17592186086459 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186086454 1075 17592186525359 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186086454 1075 17592186525431 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186086454 1075 17592186525626 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186086454 1075 17592186525635 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186086454 1075 17592186525654 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186086454 1078 17592186525635 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186087711 1038 17592186526035 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186088065 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186088065 1071 "Express the equation of the line $y =-\\frac{1}{5}x + \\frac{1}{2}$ in standard form. {{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186088065 1071 "Express the equation of the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{2}$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186088065 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}x+y=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}x+y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}x+y) & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 2x+10y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x+10y=5.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186088300 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186088300 1071 "Write $y = 2x - 10$ in standard form." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186088300 1071 "Write $y=2x-10$ in standard form." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186088300 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-2x+y=-10\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-10 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-10) \\\\ 2x-y & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=10.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186090592 1075 17592186090593 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186090592 1075 17592186090594 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186090592 1075 17592186090595 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186090592 1075 17592186090596 13194139535680 17592186045417 false]