2186045417 false] [17592186097156 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({11}^{x}-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={11}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡11-5(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={11}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡11-\\frac{5}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186097156 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({11}^{x}-5ln⁡x) \\\\  & ={11}^{x}\\cdot ln⁡11-5(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={11}^{x}ln⁡11-\\frac{5}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186097157 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186097157 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=-5{e}^{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186097157 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y =-5e^{x} + 2\\ln x$." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186097157 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5{e}^{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}+2(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186097817 1064 "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{\\operatorname{ln}a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186097817 1064 "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}⁡x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186100710 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=2f(x)+2$.\n\n![](images/24/243f8af2f2fe8e826866c228c5662baca03da66407e3cc7ba435ce80d3a9a7fe.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186100710 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=2f(x)+2.$\n\n![](images/24/243f8af2f2fe8e826866c228c5662baca03da66407e3cc7ba435ce80d3a9a7fe.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186100710 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=2f(x)+2\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ce/cef2e07bd22dab7d69f51a0197960bcdd980d5412ee47237b387dad90a4655f9.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x) + 2$.\n\n![](images/a5/a55f6aecd289cc144e621d8ed7e298d51af50523d06b7455cbec9cb3fb58dd55.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186100710 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x).$\n\n![](images/ce/cef2e07bd22dab7d69f51a0197960bcdd980d5412ee47237b387dad90a4655f9.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x) + 2.$\n\n![](images/a5/a55f6aecd289cc144e621d8ed7e298d51af50523d06b7455cbec9cb3fb58dd55.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186100785 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)$.\n\n![](images/76/76f9c7149797a0de54476f248f2ca0091bd585c5eb6d5a630b8262baa04e9fd6.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186100785 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right).$\n\n![](images/76/76f9c7149797a0de54476f248f2ca0091bd585c5eb6d5a630b8262baa04e9fd6.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186100785 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= f(2x + 2) = f(2(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\dfrac12$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/de/de6bb357ea6f3b8e7e797d914759f62f4b33629644af64a4a6cd66e7a969488d.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/ba/ba287d0600a5c0536a36e492fe25e70540f0cc7087655d7a61c190ddd42ccca9.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186100785 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2x+2)=f(2(x+1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\dfrac12$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2x\\right).$\n\n![](images/de/de6bb357ea6f3b8e7e797d914759f62f4b33629644af64a4a6cd66e7a969488d.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right).$\n\n![](images/ba/ba287d0600a5c0536a36e492fe25e70540f0cc7087655d7a61c190ddd42ccca9.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186100786 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Find the graph of $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)$?\n\n![](images/6b/6b888df08fa6579335b74eb9cb0ead944298ea8b153765249855a8b304b36289.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186100786 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Find the graph of $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)?$\n\n![](images/6b/6b888df08fa6579335b74eb9cb0ead944298ea8b153765249855a8b304b36289.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186100786 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=5f\\left(\\dfrac12x\\right)\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = 2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/49/49614f79caf13a140495c49e8a77e69aac506d03baa23849ea3806118b9046d2.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)$.\n\n!