know that $e^{\\ln(4x+5)} = 4x+5.$ \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+5 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 4x & ={e}^{3}-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110504 1071 "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13$." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186110504 1071 "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110504 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\ln (5x + 1) - 2 &= 13 \\\\\n3\\ln (5x + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n\\ln (5x + 1) &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e$, so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (5x + 1) &= 5 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (5x + 1)} &= e^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 1 &= e^{5} \\\\\n5x &= e^{5} - 1 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{e^{5} - 1}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186110504 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)-2 & =13 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+1 & ={e}^{5} \\\\ 5x & ={e}^{5}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{5}-1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110505 1071 "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\ln(1-2x) + 5\\ln(1-2x) = 4$." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186110505 1071 "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\ln(1-2x) + 5\\ln(1-2x) = 4.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110505 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - 2x) + 5\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n(1 + 5)\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n6\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n\\ln (1 - 2x) &= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e$, so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - 2x) &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\ne^{\\ln (1 - 2x)} &= e^{2/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln(1-2x)} = 1-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2x &= e^{2/3} \\\\\n1 - 2x &= \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} \\\\\n- 2x &= \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} - 1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} - 1}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-\\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} + 1}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1 - \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186110505 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ (1+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)} & ={e}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln(1-2x)} = 1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2x & ={e}^{2/3} \\\\ 1-2x & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}} \\\\ -2x & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}-1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}+1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110607 1038 17592186526005 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110607 1038 17592186526009 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110608 1038 17592186526100 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110881 1050 420.0 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110938 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110938 1071 "Find $x$ such that $\\ln (5x) = 5$." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186110938 1071 "Find $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=5.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186110938 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ={e}^{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111369 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111369 1071 "If $\\ln x = 1 - 2\\ln x$, then what is the value of $x$?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186111369 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111369 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (1+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111397 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111397 1071 "Find the exact solution to $4\\ln (3 - 7x) + 8 = 24$." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186111397 1071 "Find the exact solution to $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)+8=24.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111397 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)+8 & =24 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =16 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)}=3-7x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-7x & ={e}^{4} \\\\ -7x & ={e}^{4}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-3}{-7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3-{e}^{4}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186111882 1064 "To solve logarithmic equations containing the natural logarithm $(\\ln)$, we can use the same method as usual: first isolate the logarithm, and then exponentiate both sides of the equation using the same base as the logarithm.\n\nThe key is to remember that the base of the natural logarithm is $e$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve for $x$ in the equation $\\ln x = 2$.\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e$. So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln x &= 2 \\\\\ne^{\\ln x} &= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln x} = x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=e^2$." 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186111882 1064 "To solve logarithmic equations containing the natural logarithm $(\\ln),$ we can use the same method as usual: first isolate the logarithm, and then exponentiate both sides of the equation using the same base as the logarithm.\n\nThe key is to remember that the base of the natural logarithm is $e.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve for $x$ in the equation $\\ln x = 2.$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln x} = x.$ \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=e^2.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186112446 1075 17592186112447 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186112446 1075 17592186112448 13194139535680 17592186045417 false] [17592186112446 1075 17592186112449 13194139535680 1759218