0 1075 17592186525823 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525980 1076 17592186045493 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525980 1077 "selection" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525980 1078 17592186525353 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525980 1079 #uuid "fe0f43fe-17e5-5ba1-8867-16d28df1dad5" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1075 17592186525208 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1075 17592186525242 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1075 17592186525430 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1075 17592186525604 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1075 17592186525701 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1076 17592186045493 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1077 "selection" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1078 17592186525604 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525981 1079 #uuid "fed4a604-6986-5caa-a64a-604acfb10d3a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1075 17592186525369 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1075 17592186525448 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1075 17592186525555 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1075 17592186525560 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1075 17592186525818 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1076 17592186045493 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1077 "selection" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1078 17592186525369 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525982 1079 #uuid "ffb0e851-b32c-55d6-a987-0e041012f9ec" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525983 1068 17592186525980 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525983 1069 "q-87172" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525983 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525983 1071 "![](images/a8/a820aee214e9b47f35964e31e11a45b30d909c04bfd8b81a8ea0dc70055c2094.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525983 1072 #uuid "00f70d95-0a5c-50e2-bf6d-4e4f4b73ad51" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525983 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](images/ef/efac5abdc5ec66c794f01c53d14b10f51ff527699eae40d2f6d5802f7b51a15e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/76/76182965d505e8c87d15b65569c6a67f9e6e0baedbbf18ece2ff59feb85efd69.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525984 1068 17592186525907 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525984 1069 "q-178268" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525984 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525984 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525984 1072 #uuid "01fe6b4b-89e7-5f7d-85f4-05ba3856f335" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525984 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2+2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525851 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525853 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525874 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525880 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525909 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525945 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525947 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525949 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525955 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525958 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1068 17592186525972 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1069 "q-363219" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{30} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1072 #uuid "03ae1482-39ea-5fad-a59a-df13068a2393" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525985 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-1)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525986 1068 17592186525982 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525986 1069 "q-114700" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525986 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525986 1071 "$\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-1]\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525986 1072 #uuid "0dd7b4b0-db82-575a-9dad-d6a7735e5596" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525986 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})=\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-1]\\text{d}x & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1]-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}-1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-\\frac{1}{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]-\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525987 1068 17592186525977 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525987 1069 "q-87529" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525987 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525987 1071 "![](images/98/98ae486b18ff78601faf8a99f5310df982a73f0403ecf43186eef947b847bed4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1))-8?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525987 1072 #uuid "0e7bd953-d64f-5fc1-9cc8-824abcf9bec9" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525987 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1))-8,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}=4$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\n![](images/b6/b6b847be6f77123d3baec5f3541caf2a151039e35a5aad68b0228508d84dbc88.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/8d/8da2b9a2d31c082c069a066709afd5346c702c75a96b840d1c6ee5d9f940bf8f.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)).$\n\n![](images/fd/fd1ee07f396bf7561dc93cf4eb5f4098bb0c465c6f4b4a83328751d7e9a65dfa.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $8$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-1))-8.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9f282fc14207257835dbf9ce368d07e68cb207a04875a64cccfe07f31ef5d32.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525988 1068 17592186525889 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525988 1069 "q-123070" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525988 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525988 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,5]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[5,7]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525988 1072 #uuid "18882139-e768-512f-b798-8450fe515622" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525988 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[1,5]$ and $[5,7]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =5-1=4, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =7-5=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525989 1068 17592186525869 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525989 1069 "q-162691" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525989 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525989 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{4-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525989 1072 #uuid "1983514f-2ffa-5869-bb28-86e01bbf3b65" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525989 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{4-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{4(1-{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525990 1068 17592186525895 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525990 1069 "q-341557" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525990 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525990 1071 "$$\n\nIf $f(x)={x}^{3}\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x),$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)\\,-$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525990 1072 #uuid "198c9de8-ff94-5b1b-9df3-bdbe404e2b21" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525990 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a product $u(x)\\cdot v(x)$ with $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x).$\n\nTherefore, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)]}^{′} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,{x}^{3}\\cdot [-\\frac{(2x)^{′}}{2x\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{2x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525991 1068 17592186525849 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525991 1069 "q-345598" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525991 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525991 1071 "A water pollution researcher measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each water sample in a region with suspicions of water pollution. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular sample.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=9,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe researcher then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525991 1072 #uuid "1b0e0b9e-9780-58a6-9804-62e273336ea3" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525991 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{81}{95} \\\\  & ≈0.853 \\\\  & =85.3%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525992 1068 17592186525978 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525992 1069 "q-87144" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525992 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525992 1071 "![](images/41/41cd1216273c3157628ffb6879f50bc41dbed5490b310f5247914b2d47e6500c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525992 1072 #uuid "1ccf6e6d-aaeb-5896-b8d7-bc61aff893e6" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525992 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](images/a4/a4ac07da53b80c9ce828e9adca5efc41635d2ca4d56b97340aafa09c824bf956.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](images/28/28fc05b5ed03768282704609a1d4c05e5c579e75bca0bd5c9f4776c460fe8ae1.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525993 1068 17592186525981 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525993 1069 "q-87147" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525993 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525993 1071 "![](images/d3/d3c459cda9cd4cf2c1cb6b0a84b66108fab84d59a87bd14ed11b6ea80cbf8212.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(4x)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525993 1072 #uuid "1dc940f3-d894-5aef-acf1-d5fe41dbd6a8" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525993 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(4x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(4x).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fe8a51d1b19bbe18f90b80692df31a01debd536a46569104b1e01a18f2bae87.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(4x).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3865abcbb54d17f0af7594c7c50de9301e795b7ac1c364b180e782aa0cf94be.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525994 1068 17592186525923 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525994 1069 "q-138810" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525994 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525994 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x}.$ Its derivative is given by \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x-1}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525994 1072 #uuid "2138c134-850c-51b8-a2e7-dbdcfd2c3d24" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525994 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{x-1}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525995 1068 17592186525974 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525995 1069 "q-72501" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525995 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525995 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,0)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(-2,0).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525995 1072 #uuid "2265e6cf-85f5-580d-8ba4-5b466bbdd76f" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525995 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-(-2)}{-2-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $-1$ and passes through the point $(0,0).$ To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-1(x-0) \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525996 1068 17592186525924 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525996 1069 "q-18503" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525996 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525996 1071 "![](images/40/400427aa7dd8446124c44355959792d8e818e3118ee60ebb315c26a4b74375f1.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ and the point $(2,0)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525996 1072 #uuid "2a9deff8-468e-536e-b35a-37a221ceafcf" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525996 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-3(\\frac{3}{2})+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525997 1068 17592186525858 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525997 1069 "q-71483" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525997 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525997 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y={log}_{2}⁡(2x+3).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525997 1072 #uuid "2b5dd752-1f0d-5a16-86ac-253b01c9d23a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525997 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{2}⁡(u),\\,u=2x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525998 1068 17592186525936 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525998 1069 "q-24216" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525998 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525998 1071 "$4{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-12{log}_{6}⁡(x)=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525998 1072 #uuid "2cf033f6-5b8b-5e8f-8d55-f65db7ada288" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525998 1074 "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of $6$ and an argument of $x.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)-12{log}_{6}⁡(x) & =(4-12){log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-8{log}_{6}⁡(x)+3{log}_{6}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525999 1068 17592186525918 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525999 1069 "q-341502" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525999 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525999 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡({x}^{2}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525999 1072 #uuid "2dec0d91-0541-5bbd-ae10-733112238fa9" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186525999 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡({x}^{2})] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-{({x}^{2})}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526000 1068 17592186525913 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526000 1069 "q-159927" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526000 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526000 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $4,\\,6,\\,9,\\,7,\\,2,\\,1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526000 1072 #uuid "2e0fcfa7-37ec-568d-b873-d7a1d1d3286a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526000 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+6+9+7+2+1=29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 29 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-29)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=6+(-29)=-23.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526001 1068 17592186525884 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526001 1069 "q-72482" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526001 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526001 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,4]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,5]$\n\nCalculate  ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526001 1072 #uuid "2eda7c70-df6a-5426-8bab-8b4a22231bc8" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526001 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[1,5].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=5-1=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we can evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{n} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=15\\cdot 4=60.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526002 1068 17592186525962 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526002 1069 "q-44539" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526002 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526002 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,4)$ and is parallel to $3x-y=7.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526002 1072 #uuid "302f16e1-b5b4-533b-be86-52df48d21b76" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526002 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $3x-y=7.$ \n\nTo find the slope of the line $3x-y=7,$ we convert the equation to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y & =7 \\\\ y & =3x-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept form, we can tell that the slope of the line is $3.$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $3$ that passes through the point $(6,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-6) \\\\ y-4 & =3x-18 \\\\ y & =3x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-14 \\\\ -3x+y & =-14 \\\\ 3x-y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526003 1068 17592186525963 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526003 1069 "q-345569" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526003 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526003 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the fourth principal component of the data?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526003 1072 #uuid "30651707-ffb3-536f-b51e-a5a6fc797509" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526003 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the fourth principal component is ${v}_{4}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526004 1068 17592186525887 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526004 1069 "q-341523" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526004 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526004 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(3x-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526004 1072 #uuid "33a6f526-b75f-5ead-a328-98ce3a61e817" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526004 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(3x-1)^{2}}\\cdot (3x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(1-6x+9{x}^{2})}\\cdot (3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{6x-9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x(2-3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x(2-3x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526005 1068 17592186525954 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526005 1069 "q-79393" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526005 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526005 1071 "Find the exact solution to $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)+3=9.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526005 1072 #uuid "33c6eec8-2b71-56e5-96cd-913d5dc6b9ab" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526005 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)+3 & =9 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)}=1-5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5x & ={e}^{3} \\\\ -5x & ={e}^{3}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{-5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526006 1068 17592186525979 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526006 1069 "q-159929" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526006 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526006 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $3.1,\\,6.5,\\,4.8,\\,7.2$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526006 1072 #uuid "33ce93bc-9b71-578c-a5f0-f916b3a30de2" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526006 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3.1+6.5+4.8+7.2=21.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 21.6 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-21.6)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+(-21.6)=-17.6.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526007 1068 17592186525939 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526007 1069 "q-159905" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526007 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526007 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈11.72.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526007 1072 #uuid "374585a1-b6ca-5c27-b592-db80b72d2e1f" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526007 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈11.72$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 11.72 \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -11.72(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈10+11.72=21.72.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526008 1068 17592186525951 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526008 1069 "q-339969" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526008 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526008 1071 "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{\\sqrt{5}}+7$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526008 1072 #uuid "3937eb65-3904-5741-a603-d6263de51574" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526008 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3{x}^{\\sqrt{5}}+7) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{x})+(\\sqrt{5}\\,{x}^{\\sqrt{5}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}+\\sqrt{5}\\,{x}^{\\sqrt{5}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526009 1068 17592186525916 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526009 1069 "q-110776" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526009 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526009 1071 "Find the exact solution to $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1=2.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526009 1072 #uuid "3a62752b-0746-55f7-b956-62f3d4e94841" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526009 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1 & =2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={e}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\ x+1 & =\\sqrt{e} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{e}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526010 1068 17592186525871 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526010 1069 "q-24884" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526010 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526010 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $4\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(20)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526010 1072 #uuid "419cacf2-3464-5479-90af-e9bdd7766d7b" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526010 1074 "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of $10$ and an argument of $5.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(20)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & =(4-2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(20) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(20)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526011 1068 17592186525855 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526011 1069 "q-344802" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526011 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526011 1071 "![](images/79/79033b7dda12c84d84f668a5e3a8eeb0be25e6ad9d7b34147826ef6bdcabdce3.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y={e}^{x-1}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$2x\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526011 1072 #uuid "444cffb1-de70-54cd-8f5f-c5135af025bc" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526011 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x-1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({e}^{x-1})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{2(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+{e}^{2(x-1)}\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 2x+2{e}^{2(x-1)} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526012 1068 17592186525915 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526012 1069 "q-112241" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526012 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526012 1071 "![](images/e1/e16ae124ce49bff01ffac74a56d29848d02afd650cb5e230a1ea3ef2ff0e7aa7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x)-12?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526012 1072 #uuid "44b29d11-73a7-53f3-9fd8-f291cef7d6f9" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526012 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x)-12,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](images/57/5707b8c5cdf4d91b3fcc2aba75242af7c8df31c8e541c4a0e06f22d8493b27cc.png)\n\n- Then, translate it down by $12$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}f(x)-12.$\n\n![](images/26/2670e9453ba10db44d72ca5a1c68c2806b9668cc3867c692f7e233a88ce98043.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526013 1068 17592186525859 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526013 1069 "q-87143" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526013 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526013 1071 "![](images/ff/ff13e17ea3c946fcefb40092176e745aeb5c71c8f616665decdae39334fc42cf.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(2x)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526013 1072 #uuid "45abf7b1-2b2c-54bd-8d58-37da877b365b" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526013 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(2x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](images/73/736295268bc4b0832b7288c71f5e6721748d86a586127be5b423d3f71a7726f1.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(2x).$\n\n![](images/b7/b7519d9dec6ab7f0d465e68a1a72068db28d7639aa4b4710ce86a1eb0083d24a.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526014 1068 17592186525879 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526014 1069 "q-70887" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526014 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526014 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526014 1072 #uuid "4890c920-695d-52f8-8b20-5f7886a5c8be" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526014 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526015 1068 17592186525850 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526015 1069 "q-48009" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526015 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526015 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ for $f(x)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)+7.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526015 1072 #uuid "48c79b64-abbc-5e4d-8dc8-b69b110491e4" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526015 1074 "First, we express $f(x)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2x)+7$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡u+7,\\,u=3-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{5}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{u} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526016 1068 17592186525881 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526016 1069 "q-87484" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526016 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526016 1071 "The graph of $y=2f(3x+6)+1,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $1$ unit\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translate left by $2$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}5)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526016 1072 #uuid "48d2170e-c4ab-503f-ae82-46d7eebc72a2" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526016 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=2f(3x+6)+1=2f(3(x+2))+1\n$$\n\nTo get $y=2f(3(x+2))+1,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Translate $y=f(3x)$ left by $2$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(3(x+2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Stretch $y=f(3(x+2))$ by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=2f(3(x+2)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Translate $y=2f(3(x+2))$ up by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=2f(3(x+2))+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526017 1068 17592186525943 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526017 1069 "q-341490" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526017 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526017 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)],$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x>0.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|}$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526017 1072 #uuid "4b717300-ca17-51b3-997a-8770eb9ed2a5" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526017 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\{arcsch[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)|}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)]^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526018 1068 17592186525926 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526018 1069 "q-87413" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526018 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526018 1071 "![](images/34/34f52b01b954b7ca3a829b43995aaf56a1def9f79b68028c55993c57b386eb30.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(4x+8)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526018 1072 #uuid "4dd5481f-091a-50a2-889d-4cab50cc426d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526018 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=2f(4x+8)=2f(4(x+2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=2f(4(x+2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(4x).$\n\n![](images/80/80978c68384f9837295ad48a8ea44beff351116c4bb1b4a6a79ecfe6f40136f4.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(4(x+2)).$\n\n![](images/eb/eb1cf555fdbd31d88d695a166ef646396bbd38a160f23331c7775bfeb1bc3820.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(4(x+2)).$\n\n![](images/89/89cd8b7a309924a5d7a4ca65539059521d07cf619f5751c400622094ba6e69e4.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526019 1068 17592186525953 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526019 1069 "q-110771" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526019 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526019 1071 "If $4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x=1,$ then what is $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526019 1072 #uuid "542b7bfd-761e-58a3-80e1-933f25032116" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526019 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (4-2){log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x} & ={9}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526020 1068 17592186525862 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526020 1069 "q-345600" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526020 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526020 1071 "A quality inspector measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each machine in a factory. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular machine.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=4,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe inspector then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526020 1072 #uuid "544f3b17-0710-5a62-83f3-a61ed0f7bcdd" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526020 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =57.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the inspector used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{52}{57.25} \\\\  & ≈0.908 \\\\  & =90.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526021 1068 17592186525906 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526021 1069 "q-87271" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526021 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526021 1071 "![](images/dd/ddb1bce0c22f83b4333c4552081cb527dde48da8dfafddb392c1cc3db12c702d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3x)+3?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526021 1072 #uuid "578fd19d-050c-5dea-a9f2-ce6923cbde5c" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526021 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/fc/fc63b6078542e010a6203b139e59c82132e08038e713a295a39377c137f5fbaa.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3x)+3.$\n\n![](images/5e/5ee1df4a6b8624f25d4de8915d38fe12eb591bd08ed183879d8e94d4721e3a76.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526022 1068 17592186525860 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526022 1069 "q-162697" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526022 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526022 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\int \\frac{\\pi }{e-e{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526022 1072 #uuid "57a29797-cc3e-5389-a7ab-86cc5d574268" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526022 1074 "$$\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{e-e{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\pi }{e(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{e}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{e}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526023 1068 17592186525854 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526023 1069 "q-159949" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526023 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526023 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{14},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}=26.8,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}^{2}=264.8.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526023 1072 #uuid "5d776d5a-a6fa-564f-99d4-3a36bad98246" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526023 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=14$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}=26.8,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}^{2}=264.8,$ and $n=14.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{14}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(264.8-2\\theta \\cdot 26.8+14{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-132.4)+26.8\\theta +(-7){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-132.4)+26.8+(-7)=-112.6.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526024 1068 17592186525875 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526024 1069 "q-25563" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526024 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526024 1071 "If ${log}_{36}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then what is $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526024 1072 #uuid "5e67b4cc-0d2e-51f0-a128-4fd093eea642" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526024 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $36,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x} & ={36}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={36}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526025 1068 17592186525965 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526025 1069 "q-17369" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526025 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526025 1071 "Given that $y=2x-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526025 1072 #uuid "5fc69141-ff6d-5890-b291-b49f76733a86" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526025 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2-\\frac{5}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526026 1068 17592186525950 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526026 1069 "q-95764" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526026 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526026 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int (\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}+{e}^{x/3})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526026 1072 #uuid "614a8bd5-e8a9-58b1-931b-797f75dbf2a0" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526026 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}+{e}^{x/3})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {e}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\int -\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {e}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+3{e}^{x/3}+C \\\\  & =3(-\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+{e}^{x/3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526027 1068 17592186525865 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526027 1069 "q-95514" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526027 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526027 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=-4\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526027 1072 #uuid "61b524cd-3bec-51a5-bff2-353476be186c" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526027 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|{e}^{x}|\\sqrt{1+{({e}^{x})}^{2}}})\\cdot ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{{e}^{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526028 1068 17592186525967 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526028 1069 "q-87102" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526028 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526028 1071 "![](images/ed/edb2f75e0e5ba2d16afde5e335b8adc888a621d7e24720fab45a397bc6fb497d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=4f(x)+2?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526028 1072 #uuid "62a6fa3a-202f-5d9e-a258-2bf57d4dce4a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526028 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(x).$\n\n![](images/23/231d9ba40de9b7a061dd56be38b4145f3fd11b8488535957b8317c7c0e3c1cef.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=4f(x)+2.$\n\n![](images/95/95fce5c1cbcf16e036d4fe86a526c1910acd2b49dec8ac31e1641b137ef929df.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526029 1068 17592186525888 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526029 1069 "q-159913" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526029 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526029 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.8,\\,2.4,\\,6.0,\\,3.1,\\,2.2$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526029 1072 #uuid "643be908-7887-5e6d-8262-f7b785e61769" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526029 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.8+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡6.0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.2≈5.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 5.17 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -5.17(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈5+5.17=10.17.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526030 1068 17592186525948 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526030 1069 "q-108094" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526030 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526030 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $7\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)+5\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526030 1072 #uuid "68542d18-8a32-5e4e-8371-82abbbeca5f9" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526030 1074 "Both the first and the second logarithmic terms have a base of $10$ and an argument of $5x.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)+5\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x) & =(7-2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)+5\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)+5\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526031 1068 17592186525956 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526031 1069 "q-24170" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526031 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526031 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)-3\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)+3\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526031 1072 #uuid "68b55bab-dfd4-5ef5-a0ec-17580bbcfd66" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526031 1074 "Both the first and the third logarithmic terms have a base of $10$ and an argument of $6.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)-3\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)+3\\operatorname{log}⁡(6) & =(1+3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)-3\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)-3\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526032 1068 17592186525969 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526032 1069 "q-100612" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526032 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526032 1071 "If $\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3}=2$, then $x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526032 1072 #uuid "6a40236b-6da0-50ec-9ab9-4d1b2b103597" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526032 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3} & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}=x+24.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x+24 & =729 \\\\ x & =705.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526033 1068 17592186525922 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526033 1069 "q-138841" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526033 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526033 1071 "Consider the function $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526033 1072 #uuid "6c20f239-ec4b-5508-8032-829af339c105" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526033 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}({sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526034 1068 17592186525878 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526034 1069 "q-347204" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526034 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526034 1071 "![](images/c9/c91e196377aad64db49224cb5e38c1c8336171649708ec78b9cb80ebe6803128.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526034 1072 #uuid "6c56b3c3-015d-5836-a8b0-fb20b975ad9b" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526034 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite the above formula as\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡x+3{sin}^{2}⁡x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x)\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )+\\frac{(-\\pi /2)^{3}}{3}+3(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}+\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-(-\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{24}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{12}+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526035 1068 17592186525876 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526035 1069 "q-22753" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526035 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526035 1071 "Write $2x+22y=-8$ in standard form." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526035 1072 #uuid "704b5a22-e7b8-5a05-8c48-94b98e94c6e2" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526035 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nThe equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $2.$ So, we divide by $2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+22y & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{2x+22y}{2} & =\\frac{-8}{2} \\\\ x+11y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+11y=-4.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526036 1068 17592186525912 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526036 1069 "q-18497" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526036 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526036 1071 "![](images/4a/4ae08104573cd8a96c47ae6d98993998622617c3dd932516055cd2b0cd4e55be.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the points on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x$ that are closest to the point $(2,0).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526036 1072 #uuid "7063b461-98a7-5c7e-afa4-7c0e6c07c12e" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526036 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =y+4+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526037 1068 17592186525937 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526037 1069 "q-95818" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526037 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526037 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526037 1072 #uuid "719c2d52-6292-5d90-9bd8-199e2cf3d54d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526037 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526038 1068 17592186525897 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526038 1069 "q-95829" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526038 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526038 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\int -\\frac{7x}{8x-8{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526038 1072 #uuid "71d6f25e-3ed3-51ba-912e-8201182714bc" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526038 1074 "$$\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{7x}{8x-8{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{7x}{8x(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{7}{8(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526039 1068 17592186525863 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526039 1069 "q-113470" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526039 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526039 1071 "![](images/31/311dbd0e9cd00f766e9b83cff03c770e7c401ac576b50f43c889f5442f4a3436.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(x-1)-4?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526039 1072 #uuid "721a221e-699a-5602-87be-07d47c9a3e55" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526039 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x-1)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- translate it to the right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/99/99629999257c08d4524c865b091111e1a5f13e02384bdec8b904612063fe8269.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/16/1628b24115b5a674353600ca2e09eff3e2a9c9b8f963811577be5b9d56065ccb.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x-1)-4.$\n\n![](images/65/650e8fb922740060821e21a2f64ff28f1a6990c5187dba4c355ff0c28df1d5d0.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526040 1068 17592186525885 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526040 1069 "q-344818" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526040 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526040 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x+7}+1$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526040 1072 #uuid "72e603ef-4823-5f36-9dda-6b01a7eedbc5" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526040 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x+7}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6x+7}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+6x+7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+6x+7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=-1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526041 1068 17592186525901 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526041 1069 "q-345566" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526041 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526041 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526041 1072 #uuid "7686a2d1-5498-5a0d-a8d1-3fd56d01887b" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526041 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is ${v}_{1}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526042 1068 17592186525966 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526042 1069 "q-123067" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526042 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526042 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,3]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[3,6]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526042 1072 #uuid "77ce5f28-3326-5c6e-a417-40662b67aeff" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526042 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[1,3]$ and $[3,6]$ respectively. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =3-1=2, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =6-3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526043 1068 17592186525899 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526043 1069 "q-87483" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526043 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526043 1071 "![](images/60/601301adb254953bec91e126b2ff377864211ba047c741148e005c11682954c2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}x-1)+2?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526043 1072 #uuid "78476838-2c24-583d-9617-bd1c106f54aa" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526043 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}x-1)+2=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x).$\n\n![](images/b8/b85eed09d973abb632eb92a3a9f2abb7a2c9aa09061778d9df22eac5d59ee86e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)).$\n\n![](images/d0/d03aa20f465906c8cbcc65a1f42b208d7f0071732d47598158fc1c81ee6222ef.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3)).$\n\n![](images/d2/d2ccc316205b537c9a457b81b9fb3ba88e63602bd0aaebc90f7cedfef05863cd.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(\\frac{1}{3}(x-3))+2.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3e358c3c3d2371b5c0af932857bf34065a079d223a0d1fdfcbd09cab32a8506.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526044 1068 17592186525872 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526044 1069 "q-20073" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526044 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526044 1071 "![](images/c2/c2ba745f9f5f495ae177be1c93926fc74dffab6e69b3035cda2e7ed273f4dd05.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the point $(1,1)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526044 1072 #uuid "7941b15a-2aa3-5ad8-bfe5-4cdf148e4806" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526044 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{1}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-(\\frac{1}{2})+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526045 1068 17592186525873 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526045 1069 "q-345605" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526045 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526045 1071 "A nutritionist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each client in a group under a specific diet. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526045 1072 #uuid "7dcc2367-ae8e-5075-82b4-bcd5d8410a2a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526045 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2} \\\\  & =50.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{45}{50.36} \\\\  & ≈0.894 \\\\  & =89.4%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526046 1068 17592186525900 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526046 1069 "q-45177" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526046 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526046 1071 "![](images/71/71d77d6d9dda343a95b44fa60afbfee68619aa59bf9c6ba70eb229d072bf9a73.png)\n\nIn terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons $P$ and $Q$ congruent?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526046 1072 #uuid "7fb7b6f3-9396-53a9-a0b3-7b8a7a56bc38" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526046 1074 "The polygons $P$ and $Q$ are congruent because $Q$ is the image of $P$ under the action of translation by $5$ units to the right and $5$ units down.\n\n![](images/15/15120f2beae5043dd439f62bbc376f9b660ec97cf2541c5e2cb28e363ceed202.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526047 1068 17592186525940 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526047 1069 "q-123075" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526047 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526047 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[-3,-1]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[-2,1]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}-2({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526047 1072 #uuid "84ef10c5-43f7-5edc-8718-6907ea19be0b" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526047 1074 "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[-2,1].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=1-(-2) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{n} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}\\,+\\,{x}_{0})({x}_{1}\\,-\\,{x}_{0})\\,+\\,({x}_{2}\\,+\\,{x}_{1})({x}_{2}\\,-\\,{x}_{1})\\,+\\,⋯\\,+\\,({x}_{m}\\,+\\,{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}\\,-\\,{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=(-2)\\cdot (-8)\\cdot 3=48.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526048 1068 17592186525882 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526048 1069 "q-348918" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526048 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526048 1071 "![](images/28/28be65f566e93427105af6c87c46fa51f1a473363247f35a4aa481b93dc70090.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526048 1072 #uuid "85abb599-4be2-577d-9d0b-59402cc7e2bf" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526048 1074 "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))={sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}))-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =3(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526049 1068 17592186525973 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526049 1069 "q-149632" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526049 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526049 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,3]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[3,5]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}0.5({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526049 1072 #uuid "8659922e-fb1c-5be1-95b0-fc0ad4f5b044" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526049 1074 "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n0.5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[1,3].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=3-1 & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $j,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})Δ{y}_{1}+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})Δ{y}_{2}+⋯+({y}_{n}+{x}_{n-1})Δ{y}_{n} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}\\,+\\,{y}_{0})({y}_{1}\\,-\\,{y}_{0})\\,+\\,({y}_{2}\\,+\\,{y}_{1})({y}_{2}\\,-\\,{y}_{1})\\,+\\,⋯\\,+\\,({y}_{n}\\,+\\,{y}_{n-1})({y}_{n}\\,-\\,{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =({y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2})+({y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{n-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}}-{y}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}^{2}+{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n0.5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}=0.5\\cdot 2\\cdot 16=16.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526050 1068 17592186525934 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526050 1069 "q-162689" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526050 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526050 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{9-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526050 1072 #uuid "87d24960-3146-517f-bb76-d6b48812abfc" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526050 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{9-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{9(1-{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}}\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526051 1068 17592186525847 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526051 1069 "q-159911" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526051 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526051 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}=18.86.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526051 1072 #uuid "8b9c7eb4-449e-5e01-aa81-9a5a5e88c0e8" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526051 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈18.86$ and $n=15.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 18.86 \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -18.86(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈15+18.86=33.86.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526052 1068 17592186525883 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526052 1069 "q-112621" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526052 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526052 1071 "![](images/b2/b2c3640581329789441015f744b3dd4fc06f5178a9044a844eefc1b8616f4ea4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-3))?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526052 1072 #uuid "8caa20be-200b-5ed3-94a3-d69e22805151" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526052 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}=4$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\n![](images/8a/8a427e6cf526ce00e227e5ffeae3a21bab23fe17d1f427c8419fe3c894a761b5.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)).$\n\n![](images/cd/cdaaa955e3848d06106737098260b218752aa2a82a7aa060435ff311ced57f34.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526053 1068 17592186525935 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526053 1069 "q-112771" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526053 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526053 1071 "![](images/bd/bd8a3ad5df3c4abe46a4b5612538092e549d1197f339494be7d263511d49b856.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(3x)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526053 1072 #uuid "92353cf3-a277-5833-88a6-7c862b020fda" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526053 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(3x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/f6/f6743a24307e16fbe5df98bf2ec3a34c5aa5673e2255cdfbd2aa65c9cc9e6aee.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(3x).$\n\n![](images/a8/a88eb4b9d68be3b491a00fc3ed9a738a6a2703f3658bbc131e8348c9498a0bfa.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526054 1068 17592186525857 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526054 1069 "q-100563" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526054 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526054 1071 "If ${log}_{7}⁡(a-3)=4$, then $a=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526054 1072 #uuid "9324f898-b949-55cf-ad73-91b42fed29fc" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526054 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(a-3) & =4 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)} & ={7}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)}=a-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3 & ={7}^{4} \\\\ a-3 & =2401 \\\\ a & =2404.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526055 1068 17592186525957 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526055 1069 "q-112935" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526055 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526055 1071 "![](images/70/70c4581f3b802172e5d66a514eda3a37293f7ab2bf65ef6dcccb8073832e5f96.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=2f(x-3)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526055 1072 #uuid "9424b3c1-d470-5046-9c40-b1dfb0dc13d1" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526055 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x-3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it right by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(x-3).$\n\n![](images/c9/c9ac259b9e9a907a8c00053bdb1818f44226f126b3544358dc98c1329a5617af.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x-3).$\n\n![](images/75/757800fd367f695175be1cb991b62ae0a576eec9ee1e0018a08d56c945ec6de7.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526056 1068 17592186525921 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526056 1069 "q-345556" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526056 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526056 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526056 1072 #uuid "94503ed0-de40-59e8-a833-79a2a877bd96" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526056 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526057 1068 17592186525964 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526057 1069 "q-162696" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526057 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526057 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\int \\frac{5x}{4x-4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526057 1072 #uuid "95b5596f-d8f3-581f-b312-cc7a79146188" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526057 1074 "$$\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{4x-4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{5x}{4x(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{4(1-{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526058 1068 17592186525976 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526058 1069 "q-162694" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526058 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526058 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate $\\int -\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526058 1072 #uuid "983bd6eb-5081-55a8-bed1-50ddec8a93e0" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526058 1074 "$$\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2x}{x({x}^{2}-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526059 1068 17592186525852 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526059 1069 "q-112243" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526059 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526059 1071 "![](images/8d/8de05ebf2b8fa0939267ff7d5d8eab21ba776cf6d014db23f7502cd5ce113788.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=4f(x)+7?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526059 1072 #uuid "9a20fdf0-e060-5815-bab2-1a7f6ad5204d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526059 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(x)+7,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(x).$\n\n![](images/17/178f32ee899fe88c09207e080727689f151fa17b1fb5591f2d689e90dcf4e46e.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $7$ units to get the graph of $y=4f(x)+7.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c1e620fbb2d3de5bb27519464b931aa9d7bce9043e2f9e1a33d52897849e31d.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526060 1068 17592186525902 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526060 1069 "q-211283" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526060 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526060 1071 "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x}$ and the point $P(4,0)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526060 1072 #uuid "9e2d86f5-ab99-5ef8-9f2c-0675ddcc3d5d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526060 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+6x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-2(1)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1-2+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526061 1068 17592186525944 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526061 1069 "q-164542" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526061 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526061 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1,\\,-\\infty <y<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526061 1072 #uuid "a09c6c30-99d7-5001-884d-f495ecae121d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526061 1074 "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperbolic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperbolic cylinder whose axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis and $a=1,\\,c=\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526062 1068 17592186525970 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526062 1069 "q-45302" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526062 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526062 1071 "![](images/8d/8d89477b4c1f7b976f86d1cc5d77c1729826358e1c1367e7982ccd59970c2ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the following combinations of transformations map the triangle $ABC$ to a congruent triangle?\n\nI. A reflection in the $x$-axis, followed by a rotation of ${60}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the point $A.$\n\nII. A translation of $2$ units up, followed by a reflection in the line $\\overset{⟷}{BC}.$\n\nIII. A rotation of ${15}^{∘}$ clockwise about a given point $B,$ followed by a translation of $3$ units up." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526062 1072 #uuid "a228790b-494e-5666-af68-646e1b5c23fb" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526062 1074 "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n\n- Rotations\n\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Combination I maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a reflection, followed by a rotation) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination II maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a translation, followed by a reflection) are rigid motions.\n\n- Combination III maps $ABC$ to a congruent triangle. Indeed, both transformations (a rotation, followed by a translation) are rigid motions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526063 1068 17592186525866 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526063 1069 "q-159915" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526063 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526063 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.7,\\,2.4,\\,3.1,\\,1.4$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526063 1072 #uuid "a4ca9b4d-e32d-5442-96f5-2f609cd2145b" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526063 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.4≈2.87\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 2.87 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -2.87(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈4+2.87=6.87.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526064 1068 17592186525960 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526064 1069 "q-345546" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526064 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526064 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526064 1072 #uuid "a56a93f9-03e9-5541-a7b2-0bb8dffd858c" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526064 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526065 1068 17592186525941 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526065 1069 "q-159959" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526065 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526065 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $2.5,\\,0.2,\\,-1.1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526065 1072 #uuid "a72b9dd5-5d41-5e15-9611-0fb245235173" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526065 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2.5+0.2+(-1.1) \\\\  & =1.6 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(2.5)^{2}+(0.2)^{2}+(-1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(7.5-2\\theta \\cdot (1.6)+3{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-3.75)+(1.6)\\theta +(-1.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-3.75+(1.6)+(-1.5)=-3.65.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526066 1068 17592186525959 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526066 1069 "q-344800" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526066 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526066 1071 "![](images/5b/5b73cc737e5a07d6517431a41ad81d478e54c963b8295d4fb0996b6a33a3b34e.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$2x\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526066 1072 #uuid "ac677013-1927-5613-9941-c7a36ba6abf3" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526066 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526067 1068 17592186525931 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526067 1069 "q-340112" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526067 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526067 1071 "Given the function $y=-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526067 1072 #uuid "ada1f0d5-c981-59b4-bce0-235ff7c6a380" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526067 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{log}_{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526068 1068 17592186525893 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526068 1069 "q-313244" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526068 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526068 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2)-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(3x-2),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{3x-2}(1\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526068 1072 #uuid "ada52c09-5dae-5593-aecd-e855669a9070" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526068 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2)-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(3x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2))\\,-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(3x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x-2}-(-\\frac{1}{(3x-2)\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}}})\\cdot (3x-2)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x-2}\\,+\\,\\frac{1}{(3x-2)\\sqrt{1-(3x-2)^{2}}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x-2}(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\,1-(3x-2)^{2}\\,}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526069 1068 17592186525870 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526069 1069 "q-87487" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526069 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526069 1071 "The graph of $y=6f(4x-4)+5,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $6$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}5)$ - Translate right by $1$ unit\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526069 1072 #uuid "adbf3121-a1d5-5610-b22f-f0bc48a8db12" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526069 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=6f(4x-4)+5=6f(4(x-1))+5\n$$\n\nTo get $y=6f(4(x-1))+5,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}5).$ Translate $y=f(4x)$ right by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Stretch $y=f(4(x-1))$ by a scale factor of $6$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=6f(4(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=6f(4(x-1))$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=6f(4(x-1))+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526070 1068 17592186525961 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526070 1069 "q-95542" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526070 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526070 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526070 1072 #uuid "ae0e06e0-ba79-5c50-9e53-bfbd25cee78e" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526070 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡{x}^{3})-\\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({x}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot ({x}^{3})^{′}-\\frac{3}{4}(4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{6}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{6}}-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526071 1068 17592186525919 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526071 1069 "q-159916" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526071 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526071 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.3,\\,2.9,\\,3.3,\\,4.5$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526071 1072 #uuid "b1c528bb-28eb-5c70-b173-6d36e7f34b5d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526071 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.9+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.5≈4.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 4.03 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -4.03(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈4+4.03=8.03.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526072 1068 17592186525925 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526072 1069 "q-344817" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526072 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526072 1071 "![](images/d0/d0bfd04a904f0619093e8b6d0103f1a03c82aa2b182693fa094238667e66dd2e.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{5x+4}$ that is closest to the point $(2,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526072 1072 #uuid "b3c46934-91bf-5aa2-b420-96168ea220bf" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526072 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{5x+4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5x+4})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+5x+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+5x+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526073 1068 17592186525911 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526073 1069 "q-341533" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526073 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526073 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡({x}^{2}-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526073 1072 #uuid "b997ab13-f489-5c01-b9dd-74963fbbdc33" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526073 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cotangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡({x}^{2}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\cdot {({x}^{2}-1)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}(2-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x(2-{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526074 1068 17592186525914 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526074 1069 "q-315643" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526074 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526074 1071 "![](images/14/144e610d70eaa52a62e4bbb2de2cd9dd9ed287a87a862d48f44cb7a161c7ee26.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+3$ and the point $(4,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526074 1072 #uuid "bca0e45d-f143-5b0f-9307-2c5d5a1f757d" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526074 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=3$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(3)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-6(3)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526075 1068 17592186525904 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526075 1069 "q-112430" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526075 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526075 1071 "![](images/d8/d8959581758dcd1adc9a2730eb803c960ef16f4791cf4d37d28953d9c5bbcc00.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=3f(x)+5?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526075 1072 #uuid "bd264153-a0f2-5d3c-9c00-d5269ef8bbad" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526075 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=3f(x).$\n\n![](images/3a/3a214545d208f9ed577c7240f2f025729f9ab8e6354d1a19e8917671de7f5095.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=3f(x)+5.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51fd0e1cc6b05182cca61b97a46970ab456dd9ba2ddebb0ba9c6d197f698a13.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526076 1068 17592186525932 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526076 1069 "q-159946" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526076 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526076 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=40,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=150.86.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526076 1072 #uuid "bf364950-64c1-5b8d-a6e8-f3296448f536" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526076 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=40,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=150.86,$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(150.86-2\\theta \\cdot 40+12{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-75.43)+40\\theta +(-6){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-75.43)+40+(-6)=-41.43.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526077 1068 17592186525938 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526077 1069 "q-103460" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526077 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526077 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-3)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-1,-2).$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526077 1072 #uuid "c123720e-2a57-5feb-ae6c-82695ba41c0a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526077 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-1,-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-1}{-1-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-1}{-1+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}$ and passes through the point $(0,-3).$ To find the equation of this line, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3 \\\\ 4y+12 & =-3x \\\\ 3x+4y & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526078 1068 17592186525910 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526078 1069 "q-48000" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526078 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526078 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)+3x.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526078 1072 #uuid "c35e6115-b838-577e-80b7-a11c81db3ab4" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526078 1074 "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)$ and $h(x)=3x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=4-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{u} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=3x:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=3\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4-3x}+3.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526079 1068 17592186525927 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526079 1069 "q-95763" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526079 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526079 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int (2{x}^{7}-\\frac{1}{8|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526079 1072 #uuid "cc8743fd-7076-5d8e-abc0-564ed537d53a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526079 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{7}-\\frac{1}{8|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{8|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{8}\\int -\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{8}+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{8}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526080 1068 17592186525952 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526080 1069 "q-341726" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526080 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526080 1071 "Given that $y=(-7x)^{x}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=(-7x)^{x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526080 1072 #uuid "ce37fb31-a1bc-569c-81f3-e2b336f102ef" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526080 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(-7x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-7x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{-7x}\\cdot (-7)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(-7x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(-7x)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526081 1068 17592186525877 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526081 1069 "q-95762" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526081 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526081 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int -\\frac{\\sqrt{e+1}}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526081 1072 #uuid "d23d20e8-0932-5bfc-bc9a-09fcb1277d67" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526081 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{\\sqrt{e+1}}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{e+1}\\int -\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{e+1}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526082 1068 17592186525968 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526082 1069 "q-79381" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526082 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526082 1071 "If $3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)=6$, then $x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526082 1072 #uuid "d546754a-f994-5144-a7ef-1e397b7589a7" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526082 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)}=x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =25 \\\\ x & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526083 1068 17592186525892 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526083 1069 "q-105259" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526083 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526083 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={3}^{x}-5{log}_{2}⁡x.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526083 1072 #uuid "d56133b4-1a38-5a0f-927e-e0aa0995c936" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526083 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}-5{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{5}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526084 1068 17592186525928 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526084 1069 "q-341944" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526084 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526084 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526084 1072 #uuid "d653dfc3-332e-578a-824c-24d06ec11b4f" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526084 1074 "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\frac{2}{x})}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x}))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})+{x}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})-x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =x(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{(\\frac{2}{x})}^{{x}^{2}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{x})-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=2} & =(2){(\\frac{2}{2})}^{(2)^{2}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{2})-1) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1068 17592186525890 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1068 17592186525971 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1069 "q-340668" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1072 #uuid "d85f2141-c945-5a8d-a74b-f86923a41441" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526085 1074 "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526086 1068 17592186525848 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526086 1069 "q-344847" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526086 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526086 1071 "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}+3$ and the point $P(2,3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526086 1072 #uuid "de1d317e-6601-5a5d-9bc7-8e4f2a0ea625" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526086 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5}+3-3)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4x+5})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-8x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}-8(2)+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8-16+9} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526087 1068 17592186525868 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526087 1069 "q-159934" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526087 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526087 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{7},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=38.9.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526087 1072 #uuid "e462cf70-9c4a-5912-9b6b-fb55715b7efa" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526087 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=7$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=38.9$ and $n=7.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 38.9 \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-38.9)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=7+(-38.9)=-31.9.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526088 1068 17592186525933 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526088 1069 "q-344809" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526088 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526088 1071 "![](images/19/19ab5199ec0848b87d5ccb276e7a78ac0c7284dafb2bdeb463c15732540d43ae.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the point on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ (as shown above). Find the equation defining the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$\n\n$\\,\\,2x-2\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}} $=0$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526088 1072 #uuid "e62d164e-09b5-5d2f-b961-352e1855d4f4" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526088 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526089 1068 17592186525929 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526089 1069 "q-159928" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526089 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526089 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $6,\\,8,\\,2,\\,3,\\,5$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526089 1072 #uuid "e67f295c-595a-59e1-89e6-05c919508212" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526089 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =6+8+2+3+5=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 24 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-24)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=5+(-24)=-19.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526090 1068 17592186525894 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526090 1069 "q-341504" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526090 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526090 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\operatorname{arsech}⁡({x}^{2}-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1}[1-$\n{{field-1}} $]$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526090 1072 #uuid "e86ab85e-46b5-5037-89df-518112577fe7" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526090 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-1)+\\operatorname{arsech}⁡({x}^{2}-1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{′}}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{1-{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}}\\cdot {({x}^{2}-1)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{1-({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1}[1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(2-{x}^{2})}}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526091 1068 17592186525917 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526091 1069 "q-162676" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526091 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526091 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{|x|\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526091 1072 #uuid "e89bfa66-6d9d-5430-9f05-5264fafe0111" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526091 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{|x|\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{|x|\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int -\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526092 1068 17592186525864 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526092 1069 "q-345607" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526092 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526092 1071 "A pharmacologist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each drug among a group of potential treatments for high blood pressure. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular drug.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=1.\n$$\n\nThe pharmacologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526092 1072 #uuid "e91cdcf4-5450-5f10-aac1-2b321d212e78" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526092 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1.5)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =52.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{36}{52.25} \\\\  & ≈0.689 \\\\  & =68.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526093 1068 17592186525942 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526093 1069 "q-45606" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526093 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526093 1071 "The polygon ${A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}{D}^{′}{E}^{′}$ is the image of the polygon $ABCDE$ under the transformation $(x,y)↦(y,-x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $BC={B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\nII. $AB={A}^{′}{B}^{′}.$\n\nIII. $CD<{C}^{′}{D}^{′}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526093 1072 #uuid "e99174b2-b4df-59a0-af23-9fbd3ca32fb2" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526093 1074 "The given transformation is a rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin, which is a rigid motion. Rigid motions preserve the distances between all points and all angle measures. \n\n![](images/9b/9b250f6eda245b8a3530851d21c5438ded3fcbe9ba8cbf6db524a2c2749c3d86.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $BC={B}^{′}{C}^{′}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $AB={A}^{′}{B}^{′}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $CD={C}^{′}{D}^{′}.$\n\nAs a result, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526094 1068 17592186525920 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526094 1069 "q-159957" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526094 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526094 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=39.4,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=159.8.\n$$\n\n \nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526094 1072 #uuid "ed6e56e9-08e5-56f5-aaed-8bc6f1c64b9a" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526094 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=39.4,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=159.8,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{10}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(159.8-2\\theta \\cdot 39.4+10{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-79.9)+39.4\\theta +(-5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-79.9)+39.4+(-5)=-45.5.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526095 1068 17592186525867 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526095 1069 "q-112763" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526095 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526095 1071 "![](images/69/699dc5f983ed428e6fa6adcb3c6f32a28e24e7022784a88b425835614760c34e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(2x)?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526095 1072 #uuid "edd10633-a738-5506-bf9d-c3c2ab3f0a13" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526095 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(2x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](images/01/01de458656dbcc6b29236cf4db76c2dd1a1ee8f503c269759b25e13f7756def0.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(2x).$\n\n![](images/e4/e496df922bcae419f75857f7774212299ea5045c524c9b5dd906022fe5a5e5eb.png)" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526096 1068 17592186525946 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526096 1069 "q-66634" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526096 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526096 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $7{log}_{2}⁡3-{log}_{2}⁡3?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526096 1072 #uuid "f2bb36c5-0fc9-5c1b-9d34-184a6674cc87" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526096 1074 "Both logarithmic terms have a base of $2$ and an argument of $3.$ Therefore, we can combine them by adding their coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{2}⁡3-{log}_{2}⁡3 & =(7-1){log}_{2}⁡3=6{log}_{2}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526097 1068 17592186525903 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526097 1069 "q-87494" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526097 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526097 1071 "![](images/4d/4d81bebc6bb5addfdd5f9006eba8cd4b5e5a13c6322495a6af3c5c02885ae6ee.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={3}^{-x/4}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526097 1072 #uuid "f355b972-2cac-5b13-a407-9bcf1a157cec" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526097 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{-x/4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({3}^{-x/4})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{3}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{-x/2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot {3}^{-x/2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526098 1068 17592186525886 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526098 1069 "q-159962" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526098 1070 17592186045486 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526098 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $-1.1,\\,0.7,\\,-0.5,\\,-0.7,\\,-1.4$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526098 1072 #uuid "f4dba462-88c8-55e1-ad6f-09af7f02eff1" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526098 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =(-1.1)+0.7+(-0.5)+(-0.7)+(-1.4) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1.1)^{2}+(0.7)^{2}+(-0.5)^{2}+(-0.7)^{2}+(-1.4)^{2} \\\\  & =4.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(4.4-2\\theta \\cdot (-3)+5{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-2.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-2.2)+(-3)\\theta +(-2.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-2.2+(-3)+(-2.5)=-7.7.\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526099 1068 17592186525861 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526099 1069 "q-344825" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526099 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526099 1071 "![](images/3b/3b68e9306032877d509bd8d0b38bac2dbf94ba4827ff501e200c51230442861b.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x$ that is closest to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526099 1072 #uuid "f5383eed-0cb1-58fc-ad37-136e38e3b0f8" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526099 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+\\frac{1}{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526100 1068 17592186525898 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526100 1069 "q-110778" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526100 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526100 1071 "If $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=7$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526100 1072 #uuid "f5e20f20-51b5-534a-8fa4-4e59ce4c58da" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526100 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ (2+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ 7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, $x=e.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526101 1068 17592186525891 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526101 1069 "q-70889" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526101 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526101 1071 "Given that $y=6e\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526101 1072 #uuid "f8867c14-c978-5609-8feb-155bfedae13c" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526101 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6e\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =6e\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =6e\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{6e}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526102 1068 17592186525930 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526102 1069 "q-344829" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526102 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526102 1071 "Find the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x$ that is closest to the point $(2,-\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526102 1072 #uuid "f8e4323d-cb33-53b2-a6a3-9708cfb8812c" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526102 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{65}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{65}{4} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+8y+\\frac{65}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+8 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-4.$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526103 1068 17592186525905 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526103 1069 "q-162672" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526103 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526103 1071 "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{3}{4|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526103 1072 #uuid "f994b641-a060-5cc3-9273-f9e498c2b259" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526103 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{4|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{3}{4}\\int -\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526104 1068 17592186525896 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526104 1069 "q-100565" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526104 1070 17592186045487 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526104 1071 "If ${log}_{5}⁡(a-10)=3$, then $a=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526104 1072 #uuid "fa2d3b27-4298-5b73-9f1c-ae2a2ae60004" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526104 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(a-10) & =3 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)} & ={5}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)}=a-10.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-10 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ a-10 & =125 \\\\ a & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526105 1068 17592186525856 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526105 1069 "q-95823" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526105 1070 17592186045485 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526105 1071 "$\\int ({e}^{x/4}+\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526105 1072 #uuid "fdc2c42a-a78e-544f-8c57-a29a8369b036" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526105 1074 "$$\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({e}^{x/4}+\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{x/4}-2\\int -\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{e}^{x/4}-2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x+C \\\\  & =2(2{e}^{x/4}-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526106 1062 #uuid "0315b8e3-3795-5745-a7b2-ed382d732241" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526106 1063 17592186400240 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526107 1058 17592186526153 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526107 1062 #uuid "08124f83-7980-5571-b707-926cd78bf739" 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526107 1063 17592186134336 13194139535680 17592186045417 true] [17592186526108 1058 175